::CW_U06_L04_Q01::A poem says, "I was sad." Feedback asks for stronger imagery. Which revision best fits?{
~A. I was sad and very sad.#Incorrect. This repeats the emotion.
=B. Rain gathered on the sill until the glass forgot the street.#Correct. This uses image to suggest emotion.
~C. Sad means unhappy.#Incorrect. This defines rather than revises.
~D. Delete the poem.#Incorrect. The line can be revised.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q02::Which line break revision best slows hesitation?{
~A. Put breaks after every letter.#Incorrect. Random breaks distract.
~B. Remove all line breaks.#Incorrect. Line breaks can control pacing.
~C. Add breaks with no reason.#Incorrect. Breaks should be purposeful.
=D. Put "stayed still" on its own line after "my hand."#Correct. This creates a pause before the final action.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q03::Which style revision is most precise?{
=A. Replace "walked slowly" with "shuffled" when the speaker is tired.#Correct. The verb is precise and supports meaning.
~B. Replace every word with a longer word.#Incorrect. Longer does not mean better.
~C. Add vague words like good and nice.#Incorrect. Vague words weaken style.
~D. Ignore tone.#Incorrect. Style should support tone.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q04::A poem repeats "cold" seven times; feedback says the repetition becomes distracting. What should the writer do?{
~A. Add ten more repetitions.#Incorrect. That worsens the issue.
~B. Remove the poem.#Incorrect. The repetition can be revised.
=C. Keep the strongest repetitions and replace or remove distracting ones.#Correct. This preserves purpose while improving control.
~D. Change the title only.#Incorrect. The issue is repetition.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q05::Which explanation best connects line break to effect?{
=A. I placed the final phrase on its own line so the reader pauses before the decision.#Correct. This explains craft and reader effect.
~B. I used breaks because poems need random spaces.#Incorrect. Breaks should be purposeful.
~C. I changed it because I had to.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. I made the poem look unusual.#Incorrect. Appearance alone is not enough.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q06::Which problem is about figurative language?{
~A. A word is spelled correctly.#Incorrect. This is not a problem.
~B. The title is clear.#Incorrect. That is a strength.
~C. The line breaks support pacing.#Incorrect. That is a strength.
=D. A simile compares two things in a way that confuses the poem's meaning.#Correct. This is a figurative-language problem.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q07::Which revision best improves sound?{
~A. Add random rhymes that change the meaning.#Incorrect. Sound should not damage meaning.
=B. Add purposeful repetition of a soft sound to support a quiet tone.#Correct. Sound devices should match effect.
~C. Remove all sound awareness.#Incorrect. Sound matters in poetry.
~D. Add unrelated capitals.#Incorrect. Capitals do not create controlled sound.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q08::What should happen before revising line breaks?{
~A. Add random spaces first.#Incorrect. Random formatting is weak.
~B. Ignore meaning.#Incorrect. Meaning guides revision.
=C. Identify the intended effect or emphasis.#Correct. Breaks should support purpose.
~D. Delete all images.#Incorrect. Images may be central.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q09::Which portfolio explanation is strongest?{
~A. It is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. I changed some words.#Incorrect. This lacks detail.
~C. I like it.#Incorrect. Preference is not craft reasoning.
=D. I revised the image so readers feel grief through the window scene rather than direct statement.#Correct. This names revision and effect.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q10::Which answer is a weak style revision?{
=A. Replace a precise verb with a vague word like "went."#Correct. This weakens style by reducing precision.
~B. Replace a vague emotion with a concrete image.#Incorrect. This is strong.
~C. Revise line breaks for pacing.#Incorrect. This is strong if purposeful.
~D. Remove distracting repetition.#Incorrect. This is targeted revision.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q11::Which craft feature is most connected to where a poetic line ends?{
~A. Character motivation.#Incorrect. That is narrative craft.
~B. Plot twist.#Incorrect. That is story structure.
=C. Line break.#Correct. A line break is the ending of a poetic line.
~D. Dialogue tag.#Incorrect. That is prose dialogue.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q12::A style piece sounds flat because every sentence has the same length. Which revision fits?{
~A. Make every sentence identical.#Incorrect. That keeps the problem.
=B. Vary sentence length to control rhythm.#Correct. Sentence rhythm affects style.
~C. Remove all punctuation.#Incorrect. This may confuse readers.
~D. Add random rhymes.#Incorrect. That does not solve sentence rhythm.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q13::Which revision best supports quiet grief?{
=A. Use a restrained image, such as an untouched coat by the door.#Correct. A quiet image supports the intended tone.
~B. Add five exclamation points.#Incorrect. This may break the quiet tone.
~C. Turn the poem into a joke.#Incorrect. That changes the purpose.
~D. Remove all emotion.#Incorrect. The goal is controlled emotion.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q14::Which statement best explains style growth?{
~A. I made it better.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. I changed words randomly.#Incorrect. Revision should be purposeful.
~C. I used long words to sound smart.#Incorrect. Style is precision, not display.
=D. I used a sharper verb so the speaker's hesitation becomes visible.#Correct. This names a style choice and effect.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q15::Which is a poetry revision mistake?{
~A. Using a concrete image.#Incorrect. That can strengthen poetry.
~B. Explaining reader effect.#Incorrect. That is useful.
=C. Adding random line breaks because the page should look unusual.#Correct. Random breaks weaken control.
~D. Revising repetition when it distracts.#Incorrect. That is targeted.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q16::Which revision best improves an unclear metaphor?{
~A. Add more confusing comparisons.#Incorrect. That increases confusion.
=B. Clarify the comparison so readers understand what qualities connect.#Correct. This improves figurative meaning.
~C. Ignore reader confusion.#Incorrect. Revision should improve access.
~D. Remove all meaning.#Incorrect. Poetry still needs meaning.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q17::Which answer best identifies reader effect?{
~A. The poem is on a page.#Incorrect. This is not effect.
~B. The writer used a keyboard.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
~C. The piece has a file name.#Incorrect. This is not reader effect.
=D. The final line break makes the reader pause before the decision.#Correct. This names what the reader experiences.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q18::Which revision best preserves voice while using feedback?{
=A. Strengthen the unclear image but keep the speaker's simple, quiet diction.#Correct. This improves clarity without erasing voice.
~B. Replace the poem with a formal essay.#Incorrect. This erases the form and voice.
~C. Ignore the unclear image.#Incorrect. Feedback identifies a real issue.
~D. Add unrelated slang.#Incorrect. This may not fit voice.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q19::A poem's sound is harsh, but the intended tone is gentle. What should the writer revise?{
~A. Character motivation only.#Incorrect. This is a poetry sound issue.
=B. Sound choices such as repeated consonants, rhythm, or word choice.#Correct. Sound should match tone.
~C. Page number.#Incorrect. Page number does not affect sound.
~D. File name.#Incorrect. File name is irrelevant.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q20::Which revision explanation is strongest?{
~A. I changed it because revision was assigned.#Incorrect. This lacks craft reason.
~B. I changed random words.#Incorrect. This is not purposeful.
=C. I revised the repeated sound so the poem's ending feels softer and more reflective.#Correct. This explains sound revision and effect.
~D. I made the poem longer.#Incorrect. Length alone is not quality.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q21::Which craft area includes repetition, alliteration, and rhythm?{
=A. Sound.#Correct. These are sound and rhythm features.
~B. Character motivation.#Incorrect. That is narrative craft.
~C. Plot sequence.#Incorrect. That is story structure.
~D. Citation format.#Incorrect. This is not the lesson focus.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q22::Which revision best responds to feedback that an image is too general?{
~A. Add a vague word.#Incorrect. That keeps the problem.
~B. Delete all sensory detail.#Incorrect. That weakens imagery.
=C. Replace the general image with a specific sensory detail.#Correct. Specific imagery improves reader experience.
~D. Add random punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation does not create image.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q23::Which style issue should be revised before portfolio submission?{
~A. The final image supports tone.#Incorrect. This is a strength.
~B. The rhythm matches the mood.#Incorrect. This is a strength.
~C. The diction is precise.#Incorrect. This is a strength.
=D. Important line breaks are random and distract from meaning.#Correct. Portfolio-ready poetry needs purposeful line breaks.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q24::Which answer best matches problem to revision?{
~A. Problem: weak image; Revision: change font only.#Incorrect. Font does not fix imagery.
=B. Problem: flat rhythm; Revision: vary sentence length.#Correct. This correctly matches style problem and fix.
~C. Problem: random line breaks; Revision: add more random breaks.#Incorrect. This worsens the issue.
~D. Problem: distracting repetition; Revision: repeat more without purpose.#Incorrect. This worsens the issue.
}

::CW_U06_L04_Q25::Which response best shows Lesson 4 mastery?{
=A. I can diagnose a poetry or style problem, make a precise revision, and explain the reader effect.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill.
~B. I can make random line breaks.#Incorrect. Revision must be purposeful.
~C. I can use vague praise only.#Incorrect. Explanation needs evidence.
~D. I can ignore intended effect.#Incorrect. Intended effect guides revision.
}
